-fc3db20c7990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050020 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050020 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050020 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050020 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050020 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050020 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050020 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050020 #edb/ref 1031 "Surface Areas of Rectangular Solids" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050020 #edb/ref 1033 674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050020 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "2666651c-374d-50db-b730-cd4d8b93b51d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050288 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050288 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050288 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050288 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050288 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050288 #edb/ref 1031 "Calculating Related Rates With Squares" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050288 #edb/ref 1033 368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050288 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3d4e5778-2967-523f-a9d6-60eceb4cd1b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050645 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050645 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050645 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050645 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050645 #edb/ref 1031 "Calculating Related Rates With Cones" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050645 #edb/ref 1033 346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050645 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5bfd7882-2604-57b4-bd18-ab558a07257a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064020 #edb/ref 1069 "e-222" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064020 #edb/ref 1071 "A cube is shrinking uniformly at a rate of $3\\,\\text{mm}^3/\\text{s}.$ How fast is each side of the cube shrinking when the volume of the cube is $27\\,\\text{mm}^3?$\n\n![](images/0c/0ca7021995b1a501f6cb862b33649bdb1927f7ce4a8630a9e261382a193f40a4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064020 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c2e1a0e-4e7b-5029-94c3-3eb14d8869bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064020 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the side of the cube and $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}$ when $V=27\\,\\text{mm}^3.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV=x^3\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V = 27\\,\\text{mm}^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 27 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=3$ and $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm dt}=-3$ into the expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -3 & =3(3)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sides of the cube are shrinking at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{9}\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=3\\,\\textrm{mm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064021 #edb/ref 1069 "e-223" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064021 #edb/ref 1071 "All of the edges of a cube are increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}.$ How fast is the surface area of the cube changing when the volume of the cube is $8\\,\\text{cm}^3?$\n\n![](images/a6/a6ef2af1ed8e44f82469625e232ed67c0b809a3c8199a6ce52fd2bf2e3fcffa7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064021 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74034d81-c90c-5ab4-b27c-aa1d7aca70cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064021 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d S}{\\textrm dt}$ when $V= 8\\,\\text{cm}^3.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS= 6x^2\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}S & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V= 8\\,\\text{cm}^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 8 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=2\\,\\text{cm}=20\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nWe can now plug the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12(20)(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =480.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cube's surface area is changing at a rate of  $480 \\,\\text{mm}^2/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064022 #edb/ref 1069 "e-224" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064022 #edb/ref 1071 "Water is being poured into a rectangular prism-shaped tank. The length and width of this tank are $20 \\textrm{ ft}$ and $15 \\textrm{ ft},$ respectively. If the height of the water increases at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\textrm{ft/min}$, determine the rate at which the volume of stored water increases.\n\n![](images/ea/ea7c6749ec0585ab714640fa9155eba212e2a9c628b50cc3c3de07492b8b3aec.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064022 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6694f63e-e83a-5597-81cb-80af3c132524" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064022 #edb/ref 1074 "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the tank, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water in the tank, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=20\\,\\text{ft}, \\qquad  W=15\\,\\text{ft}, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dV}{\\textrm dt}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of the water in the tank:\n\n$$\nV = LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(20)(15)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =300\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is rising at a rate of $50\\,\\text{ft}^3\\textrm{/min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064376 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064376 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921860643