17592186045417] [17592186522684 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522684 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522684 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522684 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522684 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522684 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522684 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522684 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186522296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522684 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f82c44d4-53de-55bc-86e0-85f055a9f683" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522686 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522686 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522686 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522686 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186522380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522686 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fb6a5f66-96e9-5ca5-964a-29cec265540a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522702 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522702 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343310" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522702 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522702 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f4/f4723361163d4ca372019a91a347d2cb3d0aeee0ece1ace62762348dede9cea8.png)\n\nA worker is coating a surface with epoxy using a thickness gauge, which fixes the epoxy height at $5\\,\\text{in}.$ If the length is increasing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}$ and the width is increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s},$ how fast is the surface area of the epoxy layer increasing when its length is $12\\,\\text{in}$ and its width is $7\\,\\text{in}?$\n\nThe surface area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522702 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13a313ba-f3f4-5888-a2ed-231c29e8a4fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522702 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $S$ be its surface area. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =12\\,\\text{in}, \\\\ y & =7\\,\\text{in}, \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{in}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x$ and $y.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSince $z=5\\,\\text{in}$ is not changing, we get\n\n$$\nS=10x+2xy+10y.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10x+2xy+10y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(10+2x)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(10+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(10+2\\cdot 12)(2)+(10+2\\cdot 7)(3) \\\\  & =(34)(2)+(24)(3) \\\\  & =68+72 \\\\  & =140\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the rectangular prism is increasing at a rate of $140\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522703 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522703 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343334" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522703 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522703 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ac/ac1e157b59bb632068e45776cdeb6bdc2dee42fd7f9b92ee2ea61401c1087d29.png)\n\nA rectangular block of ice is melting in a narrow channel that keeps its length fixed at $12\\,\\text{in}.$ Its width is decreasing by $1\\,\\text{in}\\text{/h},$ while its height is decreasing by $0.4\\,\\text{in}\\text{/h}.$ How fast is its volume decreasing when its dimensions are $5\\,\\text{in}$ in width and $4\\,\\text{in}$ in height? Assume that the volume's rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe volume is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522703 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13eab2a4-15b8-5aae-888d-a5c1961fc009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522703 #edb/ref 1074 "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L,$ $W,$ and $H$ be the length, width, and height of the ice block, respectively, and\n\n- let $V$ be the volume of the ice block.\n\nNote that $H,$ $W,$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ is constant. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=12\\text{ in},\\, & W=5\\text{ in}, \\\\ H=4\\text{ in},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-1\\text{ in/h}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-0.4\\text{ in/h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the three time-dependent variables, $V,$ $W$ and $H,$ is given by the formula for the volume of ice block:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ treating $L$ as a constant, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(WH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L[W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t})+H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t})] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =L\\cdot W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(12)(5)(-0.4)+(12)(4)(-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-24+(-48) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of ice is decreasing at a rate of $72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522710 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522710 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343177" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522710 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522710 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/dd/dde167688f1693d34f07f89c923843643769369f767fec68e3578e910d8da3fb.png)\n\nA sugar cube is dissolving uniformly in water. If the volume of the cube decreases at a rate of $48\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},$ determine the rate at which the edges of the cube are decreasing when the volume of the cube is $8\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ Assume that the edge's rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe edges of the cube are decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522710 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2dec6d3d-90b6-5bbb-9401-3e793424773e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522710 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the side of the cube and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-48\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $V=8\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V=8\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 8 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=2$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-48$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -48 & =3(2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -48 & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the edges of the cube are decreasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=2\\,\\text{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522715 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522715 #edb/ref 1069 "q-30027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522715 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522715 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/03/03e31c64b26e304e720ed0178ff23af8b2ca0955c678bd695e930e0a5cb9069a.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing by $0.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}$ and its height increasing at a rate of $1\\text{ feet/min}.$ If the volume of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $10{\\text{ feet}}^{3}\\text{/min},$  how fast is its width changing when its dimensions are $2\\text{ feet}$ in length, $3\\text{ feet}$ in width and $1\\text{ feet}$ in height?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522715 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a0ddd70-eefe-5886-baf8-3017327746b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522715 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\text{ feet}, \\\\ y & =1\\text{ feet}, \\\\ z & =3\\text{ feet}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-10{\\text{ feet}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=2\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=1\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=3\\,\\text{feet}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\cdot y\\cdot z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =xy(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+xz(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+yz(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =(2)(1)(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+(2)(3)(1)+(1)(3)(-0.5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-7.25\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is decreasing at a rate of $7.25\\,\\text{feet}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=2\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=1\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=3\\,\\text{feet}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522716 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522716 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522716 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522716 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/2b/2b0876986e02941b588bf9bacbc5a0caa61eb2b95021fa4ed52408dd4f5d3f72.png)\n\nA rectangular block of clay is being reshaped as it moves through a press. Its height is decreasing at a rate of $0.5\\,\\text{ft/min},$ its width is increasing at a rate of $1\\,\\text{ ft/min},$ and its length is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{ft/min}.$ How fast is its volume changing when its dimensions are $10\\,\\text{ft}$ in length, $4\\,\\text{ft}$ in width, and $3\\,\\text{ft}$ in height? Assume that the rate of decrease of volume is positive.\n\nThe volume of the rectangular block of clay is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522716 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ad65545-bf82-508f-8a9d-4bdd8ed0f623" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522716 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $L,$ $W,$ $H$ and $V$ be the length, the width, the height, and the volume of the box, respectively. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=10\\text{ ft},\\, & W=4\\text{ ft}, \\\\ H=3\\text{ ft},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\text{ ft/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=1\\text{ ft/min},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-0.5\\text{ ft/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the four variables is given by the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ using the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(4)(3)(-2)+(10)(3)(1)+(10)(4)(-0.5) \\\\  & =-14{\\text{ ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the rectangular block of clay is decreasing at a rate of $14{\\text{ ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522722 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522722 #edb/ref 1069 "q-30020" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522722 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522722 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f8/f84ba9d0dbbf8f10fc3244c5ca3a79d4d619ab2360ac5b77fe88ddc0943dd68f.png)\n\nA rectangular swimming pool (with a horizontal bottom) is being drained. If its length and width are $25\\text{ m}$ and $15\\text{ m}$, respectively, and the water level is falling at a rate of $0.25\\,\\text{m/min},$ how fast is the water draining out of the pool?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522722 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f3094c1-9493-5020-b906-a8b3df41f18e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522722 #edb/ref 1074 "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the swimming pool, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=25\\,\\text{m},\\,W=15\\,\\text{m},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-0.25\\,\\text{m}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of water:\n\n$$\nV=LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWh$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(25)(15)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =375\\cdot (-0.25) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-93.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water is draining out at a rate of $93.75\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522729 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522729 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343210" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522729 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522729 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d0/d0bea5354a71b12e82cba561901db0fc97de873eb6f9985f0deb15afba5da801.png)\n\nA cube-shaped sugar cube is dissolving uniformly in tea. If the surface area of the cube is decreasing at a rate of $24\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ at what rate are the edges changing when each edge is $2\\,\\text{cm}?$ Assume that each edge is decreasing at the same rate, and the rate of decrease of the edge is positive.\n\nThe cube's edge is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522729 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59e7f49b-ab56-5848-a273-6624c60395ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522729 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the side of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-24\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=2\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $x$ knowing the rate of change of $S.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(S) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -24 & =12(2)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the edges are decreasing at a rate of $1\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522743 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522743 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522743 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522743 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0f/0f87cf02aac4cee2680b2045b13fc2e7ce006c39c37fa470a7803c56624d6acd.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ the width is increasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ and the $6\\,\\text{cm}$ height does not change. How fast is the surface area of the rectangular prism decreasing when its dimensions are $4\\,\\text{cm}$ in length and $11\\,\\text{cm}$ in width?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522743 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c0a4827-45c4-5e42-a7d2-ab4337115042" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522743 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $S$ be its surface area. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =11\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =6\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x$ and $y.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSince $z=6\\,\\text{cm}$ is not changing, we get\n\n$$\nS=12x+2xy+12y.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12x+2xy+12y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2x+12)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(12+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2\\cdot 4+12)(5)+(12+2\\cdot 11)(-4) \\\\  & =100-136 \\\\  & =-36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522748 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522748 #edb/ref 1069 "q-314521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522748 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522748 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8c/8c4a2802cbd06159b0b0dcbee59f5b4a6e93b4c4e029dc88d04352c50471659e.png)\n\nAn elastic rectangular prism whose height is $15\\text{ in}$ and width is $24\\text{ in}$ is being stretched. If its length is increasing at a rate of $8\\text{ in/min},$ what is the rate of change of the surface area of the rectangular prism?\n\nThe surface area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522748 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "84586114-96d4-5e09-89f7-bf9841d73d0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522748 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. The variables $x,S,$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =15\\text{ in}, \\\\ z & =24\\text{ in}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =8\\text{ in}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nS=2(x)(24)+2x(15)+2(15)(24)=78x+720.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =78(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the above expression using $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=8,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =78(8)=624.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rectangular prism's surface area is increasing at a rate of $624\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522762 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522762 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522762 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522762 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d2/d2be88ec9cb7559ca8d80d528e143d4f2dd72109c9dc576b353dd352855c53ff.png)\n\nA cube is expanding uniformly at a rate of $135{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ How fast is each edge of the cube increasing when the volume of the cube is $125{\\text{ cm}}^{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522762 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e7d3cb4-0661-5b5b-a138-b4024c524f85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522762 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=135\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $V=125\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V=125\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 125 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=5$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=135$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 135 & =3(5)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sides of the cube are increasing at a rate of $\\frac{9}{5}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=5\\,\\text{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522772 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522772 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522772 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522772 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/00/0060643c716e8f9c6d0420c419ba1d6efd7c3dc986279feb4ceae9f860dfb313.png)\n\nThe length of the rectangular prism shown above is increasing, while the $4\\,\\text{in}$ width and the $6\\,\\text{in}$ height don't change. What is the rate of change of the length of the rectangular prism if the volume is increasing at a rate of $72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522772 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b3878760-f553-50af-9094-65f6b89b14da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522772 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\,\\text{in}, \\\\ z & =6\\,\\text{in}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $y=4\\,\\text{in}$ and $z=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nV=x(4)(6)=24x.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(24x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =24(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known value into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72 & =24(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\,\\text{in}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the rectangular prism is increasing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522775 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522775 #edb/ref 1069 "q-49011" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522775 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522775 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f5/f50ef86e6663531757afb15dac45705de30ccfaae4d7079bc5a9cf474b7467f7.png)\n\nIn a laboratory, a researcher is increasing the edges of an iron cube at a rate of $0.5\\text{ feet/min}.$ How fast is the surface area changing when each edge is $3\\text{ feet?}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522775 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bacb6b56-6056-5c36-b7bd-9714bddbfa56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522775 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the side of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.5\\,\\text{feet}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=3\\,\\text{feet}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(S) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12(3)(0.5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cube's surface area is changing at a rate of $18\\,{\\text{feet}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522792 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522792 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343191" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522792 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522792 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/69/69b4b3c2488cf394df687d7446ab5a49bab037de936402f02d43196f71dddf09.png)\n\nTwo glass plates are held a constant distance apart, forming a growing sheet of laminated glass resin. As rollers pull the plates apart, the length is increasing by $2\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ while the $1\\,\\text{cm}$ thickness does not change. If resin is injected so that the volume of the sheet is increasing at a rate of $32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min},$  how fast is its width changing when its dimensions are $10\\,\\text{cm}$ in length and $4\\,\\text{cm}$ in width?\n\nThe width is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522792 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "deaa15dc-da8f-5e37-b1fc-ea24a0a7f029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522792 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =1\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=4\\,\\text{cm}$ and $z=1\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSince $z$ is not changing and $z=1,$ we get\n\n$$\nV=xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(xy) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =x(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+y(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32 & =10(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+4(2) \\\\ 32 & =10(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+8 \\\\ 24 & =10(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2.4\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is increasing at a rate of $2.4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=4\\,\\text{cm}$ and $z=1\\,\\text{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
